var e={level:`B1`,type:`transform`,questions:[{id:`b1-tr-001`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect (continuous) with 'for' + a period of time.`,original:`I started learning English five years ago.`,keyword:`FOR`,template:`I have been learning English ___.`,solutions:[`for five years`]},{id:`b1-tr-002`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Passive in the present: is/are + past participle.`,original:`People speak English all over the world.`,keyword:`SPOKEN`,template:`English ___ all over the world.`,solutions:[`is spoken`]},{id:`b1-tr-003`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' for something that started in the past and continues now: We have lived here since May.`,original:`We moved to this flat in 2019 and we still live here.`,keyword:`LIVED`,template:`We ___ this flat since 2019.`,solutions:[`have lived in`,`'ve lived in`],wrongReasons:{"lived in":`The past simple suggests you do not live there now; 'since 2019' needs the present perfect.`,"live in":`The present simple cannot go with 'since 2019'; use 'have lived'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-004`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for a longer action interrupted by a short one in the past simple: I was reading when he arrived.`,original:`I was in the middle of cooking when the phone rang.`,keyword:`WAS`,template:`I ___ when the phone rang.`,solutions:[`was cooking`],wrongReasons:{cooked:`The past simple shows a finished action; for an action in progress use 'was cooking'.`,"was cook":`The past continuous needs the -ing form: 'was cooking'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-005`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not', so the verb after it is positive: You'll be late unless you leave now.`,original:`If you don't hurry, you'll miss the bus.`,keyword:`UNLESS`,template:`You'll miss the bus ___ hurry.`,solutions:[`unless you`],wrongReasons:{"unless you don't":`'Unless' already means 'if not'; do not add 'don't'.`,"if you":`Use the word given, UNLESS; 'if you hurry' also changes the meaning.`}},{id:`b1-tr-006`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Present simple passive: am/is/are + past participle. The rooms are cleaned daily.`,original:`Someone cleans the office every evening.`,keyword:`CLEANED`,template:`The office ___ every evening.`,solutions:[`is cleaned`],wrongReasons:{cleaned:`You need the verb 'be': 'is cleaned'.`,"was cleaned":`'Every evening' is a present habit, so use 'is cleaned'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-007`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'should' + base form to give advice: You should drink more water.`,original:`It's a good idea to see a doctor.`,keyword:`SHOULD`,template:`You ___ a doctor.`,solutions:[`should see`,`should go to`,`should visit`],wrongReasons:{"should to see":`Do not use 'to' after 'should'.`,"should seeing":`After 'should' use the base form: 'see'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-008`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form describes a past habit that has stopped: I used to ride a bike to school.`,original:`I played the piano when I was a child, but I don't now.`,keyword:`USED`,template:`I ___ the piano when I was a child.`,solutions:[`used to play`],wrongReasons:{"used to playing":`After 'used to' for past habits, use the base form: 'used to play'.`,"use to play":`In positive sentences write 'used to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-009`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Enjoy' is followed by the -ing form: I enjoy reading.`,original:`I like to swim in the sea.`,keyword:`ENJOY`,template:`I ___ in the sea.`,solutions:[`enjoy swimming`],wrongReasons:{"enjoy to swim":`'Enjoy' takes the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,"enjoy swim":`Use the -ing form after 'enjoy': 'swimming'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-010`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'going to' + base form for a plan or decision made before: I'm going to learn Spanish.`,original:`I've decided to buy a new laptop.`,keyword:`GOING`,template:`I'm ___ a new laptop.`,solutions:[`going to buy`],wrongReasons:{"going buy":`You need 'to': 'going to buy'.`,"going to buying":`After 'going to' use the base form 'buy'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-011`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people in a relative clause: I know a man who sings in a band.`,original:`I have a friend. She speaks five languages.`,keyword:`WHO`,template:`I have a friend ___ five languages.`,solutions:[`who speaks`],wrongReasons:{"which speaks":`'Which' is for things; for a person use 'who'.`,"who speak":`'A friend' is singular, so use 'speaks'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-012`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Carry on' means continue; it is followed by -ing: Carry on working.`,original:`Please don't stop. Continue reading.`,keyword:`CARRY`,template:`Please ___ reading.`,solutions:[`carry on`,`carry on with`],wrongReasons:{carry:`You need the particle 'on': 'carry on'.`,"carry out":`'Carry out' means do a task or test; 'carry on' means continue.`}},{id:`b1-tr-013`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Although' + clause shows contrast: Although it was cold, we swam.`,original:`It was raining, but we went for a walk.`,keyword:`ALTHOUGH`,template:`___ raining, we went for a walk.`,solutions:[`Although it was`],wrongReasons:{"although was":`You need a subject: 'Although it was'.`,"although it was, but":`Do not use 'but' with 'although'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-014`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not difficult' is the opposite of 'easy'; use 'for' + person: This test is not hard for him.`,original:`This exercise is very easy for me.`,keyword:`DIFFICULT`,template:`This exercise is not ___ me.`,solutions:[`difficult for`,`at all difficult for`,`difficult at all for`],wrongReasons:{"difficult to":`Use 'for me', not 'to me', after 'difficult'.`,"easy for":`Use the word given, DIFFICULT; 'not easy' also changes the meaning.`}},{id:`b1-tr-015`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Never' + present perfect describes no experience up to now: I have never been to Japan.`,original:`This is my first time on a plane.`,keyword:`NEVER`,template:`I ___ on a plane before.`,solutions:[`have never been`,`'ve never been`,`have never flown`,`'ve never flown`,`have never travelled`,`'ve never travelled`],wrongReasons:{"never was":`Use the present perfect for life experience: 'have never been'.`,"had never been":`The past perfect is for an earlier past moment; here we talk about up to now.`}},{id:`b1-tr-016`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use was/were + -ing for an action in progress at a past time: We were watching TV at eight.`,original:`During the storm, the children were asleep.`,keyword:`SLEEPING`,template:`The children ___ during the storm.`,solutions:[`were sleeping`],wrongReasons:{"was sleeping":`'The children' is plural, so use 'were'.`,slept:`The keyword is SLEEPING, and the action was in progress: 'were sleeping'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-017`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: if + present simple, present simple, for things that are always true: If you heat water, it boils.`,original:`Heat ice and it melts.`,keyword:`IF`,template:`Ice melts ___ it.`,solutions:[`if you heat`],wrongReasons:{"if you will heat":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'; use the present simple.`,"if you heated":`This is a general truth, so use the present simple: 'if you heat'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-018`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: was/were + past participle; use 'by' for the person who did it. The book was written by her.`,original:`Leonardo da Vinci painted the Mona Lisa.`,keyword:`BY`,template:`The Mona Lisa ___ Leonardo da Vinci.`,solutions:[`was painted by`],wrongReasons:{"is painted by":`The painting happened in the past, so use 'was painted'.`,"painted by":`You need 'was' before the past participle.`}},{id:`b1-tr-019`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Have to' + base form shows something is necessary: We have to wear a uniform.`,original:`It is necessary for all visitors to sign in.`,keyword:`HAVE`,template:`All visitors ___ sign in.`,solutions:[`have to`],wrongReasons:{"has to":`'All visitors' is plural, so use 'have to'.`,have:`You need 'to' after 'have' here: 'have to sign in'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-020`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'There used to be' describes something that existed in the past but not now: There used to be a park here.`,original:`There was a cinema in our street, but it closed.`,keyword:`BE`,template:`There used to ___ in our street.`,solutions:[`be a cinema`],wrongReasons:{"been a cinema":`After 'used to' use the base form 'be'.`,"be cinema":`'Cinema' is a singular countable noun and needs 'a'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-021`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Would you mind' + -ing is a polite request: Would you mind closing the door?`,original:`Please open the window.`,keyword:`MIND`,template:`Would you ___ the window?`,solutions:[`mind opening`],wrongReasons:{"mind to open":`'Mind' is followed by the -ing form: 'mind opening'.`,"mind open":`Use the -ing form after 'mind'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-022`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'going to' for a prediction based on what we can see now: Be careful, you're going to fall!`,original:`Look at those clouds! Rain is coming.`,keyword:`GOING`,template:`Look at those clouds! It ___.`,solutions:[`is going to rain`,`'s going to rain`],wrongReasons:{"will rain":`Use the word given; also, 'going to' is best for a prediction from evidence.`,"is going to raining":`After 'going to' use the base form 'rain'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-023`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'where' for places: That's the shop where I bought my shoes.`,original:`This is the hotel. We stayed there last summer.`,keyword:`WHERE`,template:`This is the hotel ___ last summer.`,solutions:[`where we stayed`],wrongReasons:{"where we stayed there":`Do not repeat 'there'; 'where' already means 'in that place'.`,"which we stayed":`'Which' would need 'in' or 'at'; use 'where'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-024`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Look up' means find information in a book or online: I looked up the date.`,original:`I found the meaning of the word in a dictionary.`,keyword:`LOOKED`,template:`I ___ in a dictionary.`,solutions:[`looked the word up`,`looked up the word`,`looked up the meaning of the word`,`looked up the word's meaning`],wrongReasons:{"looked for the word":`'Look for' means search for something lost; 'look up' is for information.`,"looked at the word":`'Look at' means see; it does not mean find information.`}},{id:`b1-tr-025`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Because' gives the reason: I stayed home because I was ill.`,original:`I was tired, so I went to bed early.`,keyword:`BECAUSE`,template:`I went to bed early ___ tired.`,solutions:[`because I was`],wrongReasons:{"because of i was":`'Because of' is followed by a noun; before a clause use 'because'.`,"because was":`You need a subject: 'because I was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-026`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Too + adjective + (for someone) + to-infinitive: The coffee was too hot to drink.`,original:`The concert was so loud that we left.`,keyword:`TOO`,template:`The concert was ___ stay.`,solutions:[`too loud for us to`,`too loud to`],wrongReasons:{"too loud that we":`After 'too' + adjective use the to-infinitive, not 'that'.`,"too much loud to":`Do not use 'much' between 'too' and an adjective.`}},{id:`b1-tr-027`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Negative present perfect + 'for' shows how long something has not happened: I haven't seen him for a week.`,original:`The last time I saw Clara was three months ago.`,keyword:`SEEN`,template:`I ___ Clara for three months.`,solutions:[`haven't seen`,`have not seen`],wrongReasons:{"didn't see":`The past simple does not connect to now; with 'for three months' use 'haven't seen'.`,"don't see":`The present simple is wrong with a period up to now; use the present perfect.`}},{id:`b1-tr-028`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past continuous + 'when' + past simple: the long action comes first. I was walking when it started to rain.`,original:`He fell while he was skiing.`,keyword:`WHEN`,template:`He ___ he fell.`,solutions:[`was skiing when`],wrongReasons:{"skied when":`The skiing was in progress, so use 'was skiing'.`,"was skiing while":`Use the given word WHEN, not 'while'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-029`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will/won't + verb: If you don't eat, you will be hungry.`,original:`Study hard or you won't pass the exam.`,keyword:`IF`,template:`You won't pass the exam ___ hard.`,solutions:[`if you don't study`,`if you do not study`],wrongReasons:{"if you won't study":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'; use 'don't study'.`,"if you study":`This reverses the meaning; you need 'if you don't study'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-030`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: was/were + past participle. The house was built in 1950.`,original:`They built this bridge in 1890.`,keyword:`WAS`,template:`This bridge ___ in 1890.`,solutions:[`was built`],wrongReasons:{"was build":`Use the past participle 'built', not the base form.`,"was builded":`'Build' is irregular: build, built, built.`}},{id:`b1-tr-031`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Don't have to' means it is not necessary: You don't have to come early.`,original:`It isn't necessary to bring food; there will be lunch.`,keyword:`DON'T`,template:`You ___ bring food; there will be lunch.`,solutions:[`don't have to`,`don't need to`],wrongReasons:{"mustn't":`'Mustn't' means it is not allowed; here it is just not necessary.`,"don't must":`'Must' does not use 'don't'; say 'don't have to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-032`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base form for a past habit that is finished: She used to work at night.`,original:`My grandfather smoked in the past, but he stopped.`,keyword:`USED`,template:`My grandfather ___.`,solutions:[`used to smoke`],wrongReasons:{"used to smoked":`After 'used to' use the base form 'smoke'.`,"was used to smoke":`'Was used to' means 'was familiar with'; here you need 'used to smoke'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-033`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Stop' + -ing means you no longer do something: She stopped eating sugar.`,original:`He gave up cigarettes last year.`,keyword:`STOPPED`,template:`He ___ last year.`,solutions:[`stopped smoking`],wrongReasons:{"stopped to smoke":`'Stop to smoke' means he stopped in order to smoke; you need 'stopped smoking'.`,"stopped smoke":`Use the -ing form after 'stopped'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-034`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for fixed future arrangements: We're meeting Tom on Friday.`,original:`I have an appointment with the dentist tomorrow at four.`,keyword:`SEEING`,template:`I ___ the dentist tomorrow at four.`,solutions:[`am seeing`,`'m seeing`],wrongReasons:{see:`For a fixed arrangement use the present continuous: 'am seeing'.`,"will seeing":`'Will' is followed by the base form; here use 'am seeing'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-035`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'which' for things: The car which I drive is old.`,original:`The book is on the table. I bought it yesterday.`,keyword:`WHICH`,template:`The book ___ yesterday is on the table.`,solutions:[`which I bought`],wrongReasons:{"which i bought it":`Do not repeat 'it'; 'which' replaces it.`,"who i bought":`'Who' is for people; for a book use 'which'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-036`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Call off' means cancel; in the passive: was called off. The game was called off because of rain.`,original:`They cancelled the meeting.`,keyword:`CALLED`,template:`The meeting ___.`,solutions:[`was called off`],wrongReasons:{"called off":`The meeting did not do the action; use the passive 'was called off'.`,"was called out":`'Call out' means shout; 'call off' means cancel.`}},{id:`b1-tr-037`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Although' joins two contrasting ideas in one sentence: She was happy although she lost.`,original:`He studied hard. However, he failed the test.`,keyword:`ALTHOUGH`,template:`He failed the test ___ hard.`,solutions:[`although he studied`],wrongReasons:{"although studied":`You need a subject: 'although he studied'.`,"although he study":`This is past, so use 'studied'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-038`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not + adjective + enough' can become 'too' + the opposite adjective: He isn't tall enough = He is too short.`,original:`She isn't old enough to drive.`,keyword:`YOUNG`,template:`She is ___ drive.`,solutions:[`too young to`],wrongReasons:{"very young to":`'Very' does not show the result; use 'too young to'.`,"young enough to":`This means she can drive; use 'too young to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-039`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'already' with the present perfect for something done sooner than expected: She has already left.`,original:`I finished my homework earlier today.`,keyword:`ALREADY`,template:`I ___ my homework.`,solutions:[`have already finished`,`'ve already finished`,`have already done`,`'ve already done`],wrongReasons:{"already finished":`'Already' here needs the present perfect: 'have already finished'.`,"have finished already my":`'Already' goes between 'have' and the past participle.`}},{id:`b1-tr-040`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'While' + past continuous gives the background action: The phone rang while I was cooking.`,original:`Maria was reading. At that moment the lights went out.`,keyword:`WHILE`,template:`The lights went out ___ reading.`,solutions:[`while Maria was`],wrongReasons:{"while maria":`You need the verb 'was' before 'reading'.`,"while maria is":`The story is in the past, so use 'was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-041`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`First conditional for a possible future situation: if + present simple, will + verb. If it snows, we'll build a snowman.`,original:`Maybe it will rain tomorrow. In that case, we'll stay at home.`,keyword:`RAINS`,template:`We'll stay at home ___ tomorrow.`,solutions:[`if it rains`],wrongReasons:{"if it will rain":`After 'if' use the present simple: 'if it rains'.`,"if it rain":`'It' needs the -s form: 'rains'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-042`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for general facts: is/are + past participle. Coffee is grown in Brazil.`,original:`Farmers grow rice in many parts of Asia.`,keyword:`GROWN`,template:`Rice ___ in many parts of Asia.`,solutions:[`is grown`],wrongReasons:{"is grew":`Use the past participle 'grown', not the past simple 'grew'.`,"are grown":`'Rice' is uncountable, so use 'is'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-043`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Might' + base form means something is possible: He might be ill.`,original:`Perhaps Sara is at the library.`,keyword:`MIGHT`,template:`Sara ___ at the library.`,solutions:[`might be`],wrongReasons:{"might is":`After 'might' use the base form 'be'.`,"might to be":`Do not use 'to' after 'might'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-044`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form shows a past habit that is not true now: He used to drink coffee.`,original:`Ana doesn't eat meat now, but she did before.`,keyword:`EAT`,template:`Ana used ___.`,solutions:[`to eat meat`],wrongReasons:{"to eating meat":`After 'used to' for past habits, use the base form 'eat'.`,"eat meat":`You need 'to' after 'used': 'used to eat'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-045`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A gerund subject can change to 'It is' + adjective + to-infinitive: Swimming is fun = It is fun to swim.`,original:`Getting up early is difficult for me.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ to get up early.`,solutions:[`It is difficult for me`,`It's difficult for me`,`It is hard for me`,`It's hard for me`],wrongReasons:{"it is difficult to me":`With 'difficult' use 'for me', not 'to me'.`,"it difficult for me":`You need the verb 'is'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-046`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'going to' or the present continuous for future plans: We're going to visit Paris.`,original:`Our plan is to spend the summer in Portugal.`,keyword:`SPEND`,template:`We ___ the summer in Portugal.`,solutions:[`are going to spend`,`are spending`,`'re going to spend`,`'re spending`],wrongReasons:{spend:`The present simple does not show a future plan here; use 'are going to spend'.`,"are going to spending":`After 'going to' use the base form 'spend'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-047`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'who' clause can come after the subject to say which person: The girl who is singing is my sister.`,original:`The man is my uncle. He is wearing a red hat.`,keyword:`WHO`,template:`The man ___ a red hat is my uncle.`,solutions:[`who is wearing`,`who's wearing`],wrongReasons:{"who he is wearing":`Do not repeat 'he'; 'who' replaces it.`,"which is wearing":`'Which' is for things; use 'who' for a man.`}},{id:`b1-tr-048`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Look after' means take care of: He looks after his dog.`,original:`She takes care of her little brother after school.`,keyword:`LOOKS`,template:`She ___ her little brother after school.`,solutions:[`looks after`],wrongReasons:{"looks for":`'Look for' means try to find; 'look after' means take care of.`,"looks at":`'Look at' means watch; it does not mean take care of.`}},{id:`b1-tr-049`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'So' introduces a result: It was late, so we took a taxi.`,original:`The shop was closed because it was a holiday.`,keyword:`SO`,template:`It was a holiday, ___ closed.`,solutions:[`so the shop was`],wrongReasons:{"so was the shop":`After 'so' use normal word order: 'so the shop was'.`,"so the shop is":`The story is in the past, so use 'was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-050`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Adjective + 'enough' + to-infinitive: She is old enough to vote.`,original:`The box is too heavy for me to lift.`,keyword:`STRONG`,template:`I am not ___ lift the box.`,solutions:[`strong enough to`],wrongReasons:{"enough strong to":`'Enough' goes after the adjective: 'strong enough'.`,"strong enough for":`Before a verb use 'to': 'strong enough to lift'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-051`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the negative present perfect with 'yet' for something we expect but has not happened: He hasn't called yet.`,original:`Kenji still hasn't replied to my email.`,keyword:`REPLIED`,template:`Kenji ___ to my email yet.`,solutions:[`hasn't replied`,`has not replied`],wrongReasons:{"didn't reply":`With 'yet' use the present perfect: 'hasn't replied'.`,"haven't replied":`Kenji is third person singular, so use 'hasn't'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-052`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at an exact past time: At six I was driving home.`,original:`We started dinner at seven and finished at nine last night.`,keyword:`EATING`,template:`At eight o'clock last night, we ___ dinner.`,solutions:[`were eating`],wrongReasons:{ate:`The past simple shows a complete action; at eight o'clock dinner was in progress.`,"was eating":`'We' needs 'were', not 'was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-053`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Unless' + positive verb means 'if not': You can't come in unless you have an invitation.`,original:`Without a ticket, you can't enter the stadium.`,keyword:`UNLESS`,template:`You can't enter the stadium ___ a ticket.`,solutions:[`unless you have`,`unless you've got`,`unless you have got`],wrongReasons:{"unless you don't have":`'Unless' already means 'if not'; use 'unless you have'.`,"unless you has":`'You' takes 'have', not 'has'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-054`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past passive with a plural subject: were + past participle. Three cars were stolen yesterday.`,original:`The police arrested two men last night.`,keyword:`ARRESTED`,template:`Two men ___ by the police last night.`,solutions:[`were arrested`],wrongReasons:{"was arrested":`'Two men' is plural, so use 'were'.`,"are arrested":`'Last night' is past, so use 'were'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-055`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Mustn't' + base form means something is not allowed: You mustn't park here.`,original:`Smoking is not allowed in the building.`,keyword:`MUST`,template:`You ___ in the building.`,solutions:[`must not smoke`,`mustn't smoke`],wrongReasons:{"must smoke":`This means smoking is necessary; you need 'must not smoke'.`,"mustn't to smoke":`Do not use 'to' after 'must'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-056`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Negative form: didn't use to + base form (no -d after 'didn't'): I didn't use to drink tea.`,original:`When I was young, I didn't like vegetables, but now I love them.`,keyword:`USE`,template:`I ___ vegetables when I was young.`,solutions:[`didn't use to like`,`did not use to like`],wrongReasons:{"didn't used to like":`After 'didn't' write 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"didn't use to liked":`After 'use to' use the base form 'like'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-057`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It takes' + time + to-infinitive: It takes an hour to cook this dish.`,original:`Learning a new language takes time.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ a new language.`,solutions:[`It takes time to learn`],wrongReasons:{"it takes time learning":`After 'It takes time' use the to-infinitive: 'to learn'.`,"it take time to learn":`'It' needs the -s form: 'takes'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-058`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'will' + base form for promises: I'll help you tomorrow.`,original:`I promise to call you tonight.`,keyword:`WILL`,template:`I ___ you tonight.`,solutions:[`will call`,`will phone`,`will ring`],wrongReasons:{"will to call":`Do not use 'to' after 'will'.`,"will calling":`After 'will' use the base form 'call'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-059`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'where' + subject + verb for a place: This is the park where we play football.`,original:`That's the café. They make the best coffee in town there.`,keyword:`WHERE`,template:`That's the café ___ the best coffee in town.`,solutions:[`where they make`],wrongReasons:{"where make":`You need a subject after 'where': 'where they make'.`,"which they make":`'Which they make' does not fit; for a place use 'where'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-060`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take off' means remove clothes or shoes: Take off your coat.`,original:`Please remove your shoes before you come in.`,keyword:`TAKE`,template:`Please ___ before you come in.`,solutions:[`take off your shoes`,`take your shoes off`],wrongReasons:{"take out your shoes":`'Take out' means remove from a bag or box; for clothes use 'take off'.`,"take your shoes":`You need the particle 'off'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-061`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'However' starts a new sentence with a contrast and is usually followed by a comma: It was expensive. However, it was good.`,original:`The flat is small, but it is very comfortable.`,keyword:`HOWEVER`,template:`The flat is small. ___ comfortable.`,solutions:[`However, it is very`,`However it is very`,`However, it's very`,`However it's very`],wrongReasons:{"however is very":`You need a subject: 'However, it is very'.`,"however, but it is very":`Do not use 'but' and 'however' together.`}},{id:`b1-tr-062`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The price of' + noun asks how much something costs: What is the price of a ticket?`,original:`How much does this jacket cost?`,keyword:`PRICE`,template:`What is ___ this jacket?`,solutions:[`the price of`],wrongReasons:{"price of":`You need 'the' before 'price'.`,"the price for":`We usually say 'the price of' a thing.`}},{id:`b1-tr-063`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Ask about experience with 'Have you ever' + past participle: Have you ever eaten sushi?`,original:`Is this your first visit to Rome?`,keyword:`EVER`,template:`___ to Rome before?`,solutions:[`Have you ever been`],wrongReasons:{"did you ever go":`For experience up to now, use the present perfect: 'Have you ever been'.`,"have you ever gone":`'Been to' means you went and came back; 'gone to' means you are still there.`}},{id:`b1-tr-064`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'while', use the past continuous for the longer background action: while I was waiting.`,original:`Somebody stole my bag during my walk in the park.`,keyword:`WALKING`,template:`Somebody stole my bag while I ___ in the park.`,solutions:[`was walking`],wrongReasons:{walked:`After 'while' for a background action, use 'was walking'.`,"were walking":`'I' takes 'was', not 'were'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-065`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for scientific facts: if + present simple, present simple. If you mix red and yellow, you get orange.`,original:`Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius.`,keyword:`IF`,template:`Water boils ___ to 100 degrees Celsius.`,solutions:[`if you heat it`,`if it is heated`,`if it's heated`],wrongReasons:{"if you will heat it":`No 'will' after 'if' in a zero conditional.`,"if you heat":`You need an object: 'heat it'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-066`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Negative present passive: am/is/are + not + past participle. Phones are not allowed in class.`,original:`They don't allow dogs in this restaurant.`,keyword:`ALLOWED`,template:`Dogs ___ in this restaurant.`,solutions:[`are not allowed`,`aren't allowed`],wrongReasons:{"don't allowed":`In the passive, use 'are not', not 'don't'.`,"are not allow":`Use the past participle 'allowed'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-067`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Could' is the past of 'can' for ability: She could read at four.`,original:`I was able to swim when I was five.`,keyword:`COULD`,template:`I ___ when I was five.`,solutions:[`could swim`],wrongReasons:{"could swam":`After 'could' use the base form 'swim'.`,"can swim":`This is about the past, so use 'could'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-068`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Question form: Did + subject + use to + base form? Did you use to play tennis?`,original:`Did you live in the city as a child?`,keyword:`USE`,template:`___ live in the city as a child?`,solutions:[`Did you use to`],wrongReasons:{"did you used to":`After 'did' write 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"used you to":`Make the question with 'Did you use to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-069`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After a preposition like 'at', use the -ing form: He is good at cooking.`,original:`She draws very well.`,keyword:`GOOD`,template:`She is ___.`,solutions:[`good at drawing`,`very good at drawing`],wrongReasons:{"good at draw":`After the preposition 'at' use the -ing form.`,"good in drawing":`We say 'good at', not 'good in'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-070`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous to ask about future arrangements: What are you doing at the weekend?`,original:`Do you have any plans for this evening?`,keyword:`DOING`,template:`What ___ this evening?`,solutions:[`are you doing`],wrongReasons:{"do you doing":`The present continuous question is 'are you doing'.`,"you are doing":`In a question, put 'are' before 'you'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-071`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'That' can replace 'which' for things: The cake that she made was delicious.`,original:`We watched a film. It was very funny.`,keyword:`THAT`,template:`The film ___ was very funny.`,solutions:[`that we watched`],wrongReasons:{"that we watched it":`Do not repeat 'it'; 'that' replaces it.`,"that watched":`You need a subject: 'that we watched'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-072`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Give up' + noun or -ing means stop doing something: She gave up coffee.`,original:`I stopped eating chocolate last year.`,keyword:`GAVE`,template:`I ___ last year.`,solutions:[`gave up chocolate`,`gave up eating chocolate`,`gave chocolate up`],wrongReasons:{"gave up to eat chocolate":`After 'give up' use the -ing form or a noun.`,"gave away chocolate":`'Give away' means give things for free; 'give up' means stop.`}},{id:`b1-tr-073`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Because' is followed by a subject and a verb; 'because of' by a noun: because it was raining / because of the rain.`,original:`We stayed inside because of the heavy rain.`,keyword:`BECAUSE`,template:`We stayed inside ___ raining heavily.`,solutions:[`because it was`],wrongReasons:{"because of it was":`'Because of' is followed by a noun, not a clause.`,"because it is":`The story is past, so use 'was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-074`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Comparative adjective + 'than': He is older than me.`,original:`I'm not as tall as my sister.`,keyword:`TALLER`,template:`My sister is ___ me.`,solutions:[`taller than`],wrongReasons:{"taller that":`After a comparative use 'than', not 'that'.`,"more taller than":`Do not use 'more' with 'taller'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-075`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect (simple or continuous) + 'for' for an action that started in the past and continues: She has worked here for years.`,original:`Our teacher started working here ten years ago.`,keyword:`HAS`,template:`Our teacher ___ here for ten years.`,solutions:[`has worked`,`has been working`,`has been`],wrongReasons:{"has work":`After 'has' use the past participle: 'has worked'.`,"has working":`Use 'has worked' or 'has been working', not 'has working'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-076`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Still' + past continuous shows an action had not finished: They were still talking when I left.`,original:`The guests arrived in the middle of our cleaning.`,keyword:`STILL`,template:`We ___ when the guests arrived.`,solutions:[`were still cleaning`],wrongReasons:{"still cleaned":`The cleaning was in progress, so use 'were still cleaning'.`,"were cleaning still":`'Still' goes between 'were' and the -ing form.`}},{id:`b1-tr-077`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + verb in the main clause, if + present simple: You'll fall if you run.`,original:`Don't touch that wire or you'll get an electric shock.`,keyword:`TOUCH`,template:`You'll get an electric shock if you ___.`,solutions:[`touch that wire`,`touch the wire`],wrongReasons:{"will touch that wire":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'.`,"touched that wire":`This is a real future possibility, so use the present simple 'touch'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-078`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Present passive + 'by' for who does the action: These toys are made by hand.`,original:`A local company makes these chairs.`,keyword:`MADE`,template:`These chairs ___ a local company.`,solutions:[`are made by`],wrongReasons:{"are made from":`'From' is for materials; for the company use 'by'.`,"is made by":`'Chairs' is plural, so use 'are'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-079`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'Can I' + base form to ask for permission: Can I use your pen?`,original:`Is it OK if I open the window?`,keyword:`CAN`,template:`___ the window?`,solutions:[`Can I open`],wrongReasons:{"can i to open":`Do not use 'to' after 'can'.`,"i can open":`In a question, put 'can' before the subject: 'Can I open'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-080`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to be' + adjective describes a past state that has changed: The street used to be dark.`,original:`This town was quiet years ago; now it is busy.`,keyword:`USED`,template:`This town ___ quiet.`,solutions:[`used to be`],wrongReasons:{"used to being":`After 'used to' use the base form 'be'.`,"was used to be":`Do not add 'was'; say 'used to be'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-081`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'What about' + -ing makes a suggestion: What about having pizza tonight?`,original:`Let's go for a walk.`,keyword:`ABOUT`,template:`What ___ for a walk?`,solutions:[`about going`],wrongReasons:{"about go":`After the preposition 'about' use the -ing form.`,"about to go":`'About to go' means 'going very soon'; for a suggestion use 'about going'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-082`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'I think' + will for opinions about the future: I think she will win.`,original:`I expect it will be sunny tomorrow.`,keyword:`THINK`,template:`I ___ sunny tomorrow.`,solutions:[`think it will be`,`think it'll be`],wrongReasons:{"think it is going be":`You need 'to': 'going to be'; and the simplest answer is 'think it will be'.`,"think it will sunny":`You need the verb 'be': 'it will be sunny'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-083`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'who' + verb when the person does the action: The boy who found the dog was happy.`,original:`The scientist won a prize. She discovered a new planet.`,keyword:`WHO`,template:`The scientist ___ a new planet won a prize.`,solutions:[`who discovered`],wrongReasons:{"who she discovered":`Do not repeat 'she'; 'who' replaces it.`,"which discovered":`'Which' is for things; for a scientist use 'who'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-084`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take after' means look or behave like an older family member: She takes after her mum.`,original:`Lucas looks very similar to his father.`,keyword:`TAKES`,template:`Lucas ___ his father.`,solutions:[`takes after`],wrongReasons:{"takes off":`'Take off' means remove or leave the ground; 'take after' means be similar to.`,"takes from":`The phrasal verb is 'take after'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-085`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'But' joins two contrasting clauses: The hotel was old, but it was clean.`,original:`Although the film was long, I enjoyed it.`,keyword:`BUT`,template:`The film was long, ___ it.`,solutions:[`but I enjoyed`],wrongReasons:{"but i enjoy":`The story is in the past, so use 'enjoyed'.`,"but although i enjoyed":`Use only one linker: 'but I enjoyed'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-086`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the superlative to say someone is number one in a group: She sings the best in the choir.`,original:`Nobody in the class runs faster than Leo.`,keyword:`FASTEST`,template:`Leo runs ___ in the class.`,solutions:[`the fastest`,`fastest`],wrongReasons:{"the most fastest":`Do not use 'most' with 'fastest'.`,"the faster":`To compare with the whole class, use the superlative 'the fastest'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-087`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'have had' + 'since' + a point in time for possession that continues now: I have had this phone since 2022.`,original:`I bought this bike in March and I still use it.`,keyword:`SINCE`,template:`I ___ March.`,solutions:[`have had this bike since`,`'ve had this bike since`],wrongReasons:{"have this bike since":`With 'since' you need the present perfect: 'have had'.`,"had this bike since":`The past simple does not show that you still have it; use 'have had'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-088`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'while' + past continuous for the action in progress: I met her while I was shopping.`,original:`I saw an accident as I drove to work this morning.`,keyword:`DRIVING`,template:`I saw an accident while I ___ to work this morning.`,solutions:[`was driving`],wrongReasons:{drove:`With 'while' and the keyword DRIVING, use 'was driving'.`,"am driving":`This happened this morning, so use the past: 'was driving'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-089`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for general truths: if + present simple, present simple. If you don't sleep, you feel tired.`,original:`Plants die without water.`,keyword:`IF`,template:`Plants die ___ water.`,solutions:[`if they don't get`,`if they do not get`,`if they don't have`,`if they do not have`],wrongReasons:{"if they doesn't get":`'They' takes 'don't', not 'doesn't'.`,"if they won't get":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-090`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past simple passive when we don't know who did it: My car was stolen last week.`,original:`Somebody stole my phone on the train.`,keyword:`STOLEN`,template:`My phone ___ on the train.`,solutions:[`was stolen`],wrongReasons:{"was stole":`Use the past participle 'stolen', not 'stole'.`,"has stole":`Use 'was stolen'; 'stole' is not a past participle.`}},{id:`b1-tr-091`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'May' + base form shows possibility: It may snow tonight.`,original:`It's possible that we will be late.`,keyword:`MAY`,template:`We ___ late.`,solutions:[`may be`],wrongReasons:{"may are":`After 'may' use the base form 'be'.`,"may will be":`Do not use two modals together; say 'may be'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-092`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form for a general past habit: People used to travel by horse.`,original:`In the past, people wrote letters much more than today.`,keyword:`USED`,template:`People ___ letters much more than today.`,solutions:[`used to write`],wrongReasons:{"used to wrote":`After 'used to' use the base form 'write'.`,"use to write":`In positive sentences write 'used to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-093`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Forget' + to-infinitive is about something you need to do: Don't forget to call me.`,original:`Remember to lock the door.`,keyword:`FORGET`,template:`Don't ___ the door.`,solutions:[`forget to lock`],wrongReasons:{"forget locking":`'Forget locking' is about a memory of the past; for a future task use 'forget to lock'.`,"forget lock":`You need 'to' after 'forget'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-094`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for intentions: He is going to study law.`,original:`Lisa intends to become a nurse.`,keyword:`GOING`,template:`Lisa ___ a nurse.`,solutions:[`is going to become`,`is going to be`,`'s going to become`,`'s going to be`],wrongReasons:{"going to become":`You need 'is' before 'going to'.`,"are going to become":`'Lisa' is singular, so use 'is'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-095`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'That' can introduce a clause about a thing: I like the bag that you bought.`,original:`I lost the watch. My parents gave it to me.`,keyword:`THAT`,template:`I lost the watch ___ me.`,solutions:[`that my parents gave`,`that my parents had given`],wrongReasons:{"that my parents gave it":`Do not repeat 'it'; 'that' replaces it.`,"that my parents give":`The giving happened in the past, so use 'gave'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-096`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Break down' means stop working (for machines and cars): My computer broke down.`,original:`The car stopped working on the motorway.`,keyword:`BROKE`,template:`The car ___ on the motorway.`,solutions:[`broke down`],wrongReasons:{"broke up":`'Break up' means end a relationship; 'break down' is for machines.`,broke:`You need the particle 'down'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-097`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Change 'so' (result) to 'because' (reason) by swapping the clauses: It rained, so I stayed = I stayed because it rained.`,original:`The bus was late, so I missed my class.`,keyword:`BECAUSE`,template:`I missed my class ___ late.`,solutions:[`because the bus was`],wrongReasons:{"because of the bus was":`'Because of' needs a noun only; before a clause use 'because'.`,"because was the bus":`Use normal word order: 'because the bus was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-098`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'A ten-minute walk from' tells you how far a place is on foot: The beach is a five-minute walk from the hotel.`,original:`It takes thirty minutes to walk to the station from here.`,keyword:`WALK`,template:`The station is a thirty-minute ___ here.`,solutions:[`walk from`],wrongReasons:{"walking from":`After 'a thirty-minute' use the noun 'walk'.`,"walk to":`The station is the place; you walk to it 'from here'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-099`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'haven't been to' + 'for' to say how long since we last went somewhere: I haven't been to the beach for years.`,original:`It's a long time since we last went to the cinema.`,keyword:`BEEN`,template:`We ___ to the cinema for a long time.`,solutions:[`haven't been`,`have not been`],wrongReasons:{"didn't go":`With 'for a long time' up to now, use the present perfect: 'haven't been'.`,"haven't gone":`'Been to' is the usual form for visits; 'haven't gone' sounds unnatural here.`}},{id:`b1-tr-100`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for a longer situation when something happened: They met while they were studying.`,original:`Tom and Ana met during a trip around Greece.`,keyword:`TRAVELLING`,template:`Tom and Ana met while they ___ around Greece.`,solutions:[`were travelling`],wrongReasons:{"was travelling":`'They' is plural, so use 'were'.`,travelled:`The trip was in progress when they met, so use 'were travelling'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-101`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`First conditional for a possible future event: If I see him, I'll tell him.`,original:`Maybe I'll see Lucy later. I'll give her your message.`,keyword:`SEE`,template:`If I ___, I'll give her your message.`,solutions:[`see Lucy later`,`see Lucy`,`see her later`,`see her`],wrongReasons:{"will see lucy later":`After 'if' use the present simple: 'see'.`,"saw lucy later":`This is about the future, so use the present simple 'see'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-102`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Negative past passive: wasn't/weren't + past participle. She wasn't told about the meeting.`,original:`They didn't invite me to the party.`,keyword:`INVITED`,template:`I ___ to the party.`,solutions:[`wasn't invited`,`was not invited`],wrongReasons:{"didn't invited":`In the passive use 'wasn't', not 'didn't'.`,"weren't invited":`'I' takes 'wasn't', not 'weren't'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-103`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Had to' is the past of 'have to' and 'must': We had to wait an hour.`,original:`It was necessary for me to work late yesterday.`,keyword:`HAD`,template:`I ___ work late yesterday.`,solutions:[`had to`],wrongReasons:{must:`'Must' has no past form; use 'had to'.`,"have to":`'Yesterday' is past, so use 'had to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-104`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form for repeated past actions: We used to visit our cousins every summer.`,original:`We often went camping when the kids were small.`,keyword:`USED`,template:`We ___ camping when the kids were small.`,solutions:[`used to go`],wrongReasons:{"used to going":`After 'used to' use the base form 'go'.`,"used to went":`After 'used to' use 'go', not 'went'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-105`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Can't stand' + -ing means you really don't like something: I can't stand getting up early.`,original:`Maria hates waiting in queues.`,keyword:`STAND`,template:`Maria can't ___ in queues.`,solutions:[`stand waiting`],wrongReasons:{"stand wait":`After 'can't stand' use the -ing form: 'waiting'.`,"stand to waiting":`Do not add 'to'; say 'can't stand waiting'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-106`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for arrangements with a time and place: I'm having dinner with Ana tonight.`,original:`Mark and I have arranged to meet for lunch on Friday.`,keyword:`MEETING`,template:`Mark and I ___ for lunch on Friday.`,solutions:[`are meeting`,`'re meeting`],wrongReasons:{"am meeting":`'Mark and I' is plural, so use 'are'.`,meeting:`You need the verb 'are' before 'meeting'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-107`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'where' to add information about a place: This is the town where I grew up.`,original:`This is the village. My grandmother was born there.`,keyword:`WHERE`,template:`This is the village ___ born.`,solutions:[`where my grandmother was`],wrongReasons:{"where was my grandmother":`In a relative clause, use normal word order: 'where my grandmother was'.`,"which my grandmother was":`For a place without 'in', use 'where'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-108`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Fill in' or 'fill out' means write information on a form: Fill in your name here.`,original:`Please complete this form.`,keyword:`FILL`,template:`Please ___ this form.`,solutions:[`fill in`,`fill out`],wrongReasons:{"fill up":`'Fill up' means make a container full; for forms use 'fill in'.`,fill:`You need a particle: 'fill in'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-109`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'However' at the start of a sentence shows contrast with the sentence before: He's young. However, he's very wise.`,original:`Although she's very rich, she lives in a small house.`,keyword:`HOWEVER`,template:`She's very rich. ___ in a small house.`,solutions:[`However, she lives`,`However she lives`],wrongReasons:{"however, she live":`'She' needs the -s form: 'lives'.`,"however, although she lives":`Use only one linker: 'However, she lives'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-110`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Can't afford' means you don't have enough money for something: We can't afford a holiday.`,original:`I don't have enough money to buy that car.`,keyword:`AFFORD`,template:`I ___ that car.`,solutions:[`can't afford`,`cannot afford`,`can not afford`,`can't afford to buy`,`cannot afford to buy`],wrongReasons:{"don't afford":`'Afford' is usually used with 'can' or 'can't'.`,"can't afford buying":`After 'afford' use the to-infinitive: 'afford to buy'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-111`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect for a past action with a result now: I have lost my wallet (I don't have it now).`,original:`She lost her keys and she still can't find them.`,keyword:`LOST`,template:`She ___ her keys.`,solutions:[`has lost`,`'s lost`],wrongReasons:{lost:`The past simple does not show that the keys are still missing; use 'has lost'.`,"have lost":`'She' needs 'has', not 'have'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-112`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past continuous for the activity in progress, past simple for the event that interrupted it: It snowed while we were skiing.`,original:`It started to rain in the middle of our football match.`,keyword:`PLAYING`,template:`It started to rain while we ___ football.`,solutions:[`were playing`],wrongReasons:{played:`The match was in progress, so use 'were playing'.`,"are playing":`This is a past story, so use 'were playing'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-113`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for something that always happens: If I miss breakfast, I get hungry.`,original:`My boss gets angry when I am late.`,keyword:`IF`,template:`___ late, my boss gets angry.`,solutions:[`If I am`,`If I'm`],wrongReasons:{"if i will be":`Do not use 'will' after 'if'.`,"if i was":`This is a general habit now, so use the present: 'If I am'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-114`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Present passive question: is/are + subject + past participle? Is English taught here?`,original:`Do they speak French in this part of Canada?`,keyword:`SPOKEN`,template:`___ in this part of Canada?`,solutions:[`Is French spoken`],wrongReasons:{"does french spoken":`Passive questions use 'is', not 'does'.`,"is french speak":`Use the past participle 'spoken'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-115`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Give advice with 'should' + base form: You should buy your tickets online.`,original:`My advice is to book the hotel early.`,keyword:`SHOULD`,template:`You ___ the hotel early.`,solutions:[`should book`],wrongReasons:{"should booking":`After 'should' use the base form 'book'.`,"should to book":`Do not use 'to' after 'should'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-116`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form shows a past situation that has changed: He used to have long hair.`,original:`I wore glasses before, but now I wear contact lenses.`,keyword:`USED`,template:`I ___ glasses.`,solutions:[`used to wear`],wrongReasons:{"am used to wear":`'Be used to' means 'be familiar with'; you need 'used to wear'.`,"used to wore":`After 'used to' use the base form 'wear'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-117`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Decide' is followed by the to-infinitive: They decided to move house.`,original:`He decided he would study medicine.`,keyword:`DECIDED`,template:`He ___ medicine.`,solutions:[`decided to study`],wrongReasons:{"decided studying":`'Decide' takes 'to' + verb, not the -ing form.`,"decided study":`You need 'to' after 'decided'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-118`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for offers made at the moment of speaking: I'll open the door for you.`,original:`Let me carry those bags for you.`,keyword:`CARRY`,template:`___ those bags for you.`,solutions:[`I will carry`,`I'll carry`],wrongReasons:{"i will carrying":`After 'will' use the base form 'carry'.`,"i am going to carry":`For quick offers made at the moment of speaking, use 'will', not 'going to'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-119`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'who' for people: The teachers who work here are friendly.`,original:`The nurses work at this hospital. They are very kind.`,keyword:`WHO`,template:`The nurses ___ at this hospital are very kind.`,solutions:[`who work`],wrongReasons:{"who works":`'The nurses' is plural, so use 'work'.`,"who they work":`Do not repeat 'they'; 'who' replaces it.`}},{id:`b1-tr-120`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Run out of' means have no more of something: We've run out of bread.`,original:`We have no milk left.`,keyword:`RUN`,template:`We have ___ milk.`,solutions:[`run out of`],wrongReasons:{"run out":`You need 'of' before the noun: 'run out of milk'.`,"ran out of":`After 'have' use the past participle 'run'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-121`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'because' + subject + verb to give a reason: I was late because my alarm didn't ring.`,original:`My phone battery was dead, so I couldn't call you.`,keyword:`BECAUSE`,template:`I couldn't call you ___ dead.`,solutions:[`because my phone battery was`,`because my battery was`,`because the battery was`,`because my phone's battery was`],wrongReasons:{"because of my phone battery was":`'Because of' is followed by a noun, not a clause.`,"because my phone battery is":`The story is past, so use 'was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-122`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use an adverb, not an adjective, to describe how someone does something: He drives carefully.`,original:`Paula is an excellent singer.`,keyword:`SINGS`,template:`Paula ___.`,solutions:[`sings very well`,`sings really well`,`sings excellently`,`sings beautifully`,`sings extremely well`],wrongReasons:{"sings very good":`After a verb use the adverb 'well', not the adjective 'good'.`,"sings excellent":`After a verb use the adverb 'excellently' or 'very well'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-123`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'has been' + adjective + 'for' for a state that started in the past: The shop has been open for a month.`,original:`The museum opened two weeks ago.`,keyword:`OPEN`,template:`The museum ___ for two weeks.`,solutions:[`has been open`],wrongReasons:{"has opened":`'Opened' is a single action; for a state lasting two weeks use 'has been open'.`,"is open":`The present simple cannot go with 'for two weeks'; use 'has been open'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-124`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to explain what was happening at that moment: I didn't answer because I was sleeping.`,original:`I didn't hear the doorbell. I had my headphones on and music was playing.`,keyword:`LISTENING`,template:`I didn't hear the doorbell because I ___ to music.`,solutions:[`was listening`],wrongReasons:{listened:`The music was going on at that moment, so use 'was listening'.`,"was listen":`Use the -ing form after 'was': 'was listening'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-125`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Unless' + present simple = 'if ... not': The plant will die unless you water it.`,original:`Keep the milk in the fridge or it will go bad.`,keyword:`UNLESS`,template:`The milk will go bad ___ it in the fridge.`,solutions:[`unless you keep`,`unless you put`],wrongReasons:{"unless you don't keep":`'Unless' is already negative; use 'unless you keep'.`,"unless you will keep":`Use the present simple after 'unless', not 'will'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-126`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past passive + 'by' + the person: America was reached by Columbus in 1492.`,original:`Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin in 1928.`,keyword:`DISCOVERED`,template:`Penicillin ___ Alexander Fleming in 1928.`,solutions:[`was discovered by`],wrongReasons:{"discovered by":`You need 'was' before the past participle.`,"is discovered by":`1928 is in the past, so use 'was'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-127`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Can't' + base form means something is not allowed: You can't swim here.`,original:`Students aren't allowed to use phones in the exam.`,keyword:`CAN`,template:`Students ___ phones in the exam.`,solutions:[`can't use`,`cannot use`,`can not use`],wrongReasons:{"can use":`This means it is allowed; you need 'can't use'.`,"can't to use":`Do not use 'to' after 'can't'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-128`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Used to be' + adjective describes a past state that is no longer true: I used to be shy.`,original:`My brother is no longer afraid of dogs.`,keyword:`BE`,template:`My brother used ___ dogs.`,solutions:[`to be afraid of`,`to be scared of`,`to be frightened of`],wrongReasons:{"to afraid of":`You need the verb 'be': 'used to be afraid of'.`,"to be afraid from":`The adjective 'afraid' takes 'of', not 'from'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-129`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so use the -ing form: I'm looking forward to meeting her.`,original:`I can't wait to see you again.`,keyword:`FORWARD`,template:`I'm looking ___ you again.`,solutions:[`forward to seeing`],wrongReasons:{"forward to see":`'To' here is a preposition; use 'seeing'.`,"forward seeing":`You need 'to': 'looking forward to seeing'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-130`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous or 'going to' for future plans: We're going to the theatre tonight.`,original:`We have tickets for the concert on Saturday.`,keyword:`GOING`,template:`We ___ to the concert on Saturday.`,solutions:[`are going`,`'re going`,`are going to go`,`'re going to go`],wrongReasons:{go:`The present simple does not show a plan; use 'are going'.`,"will going":`'Will' takes the base form; here use 'are going'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-131`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`relative-clauses`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'which' for things and animals: A fridge is a machine which keeps food cold.`,original:`A thermometer is a tool. It measures temperature.`,keyword:`WHICH`,template:`A thermometer is a tool ___ temperature.`,solutions:[`which measures`],wrongReasons:{"who measures":`'Who' is for people; for a tool use 'which'.`,"which measure":`'A tool' is singular, so use 'measures'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-132`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Get up' means leave your bed: I get up at six every day.`,original:`I woke up and left my bed at seven.`,keyword:`GOT`,template:`I woke up and ___ at seven.`,solutions:[`got up`,`got out of bed`],wrongReasons:{"got on":`'Get on' means enter a bus or train; 'get up' means leave your bed.`,got:`You need the particle 'up'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-133`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Even though' is a strong form of 'although': Even though it was late, she called me.`,original:`Ben didn't have a map, but he found the way.`,keyword:`EVEN`,template:`Ben found the way ___ have a map.`,solutions:[`even though he didn't`,`even though he did not`],wrongReasons:{"even if he didn't":`'Even if' is for possible situations; for a real fact use 'even though'.`,"even though he doesn't":`The story is past, so use 'didn't'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-134`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use -ed adjectives for how people feel and -ing adjectives for things: I was bored. The film was boring.`,original:`The film was boring for me.`,keyword:`BORED`,template:`I ___ the film.`,solutions:[`was bored by`,`was bored with`,`was bored during`,`got bored during`],wrongReasons:{"was boring by":`'Boring' describes the film; for your feeling use 'bored'.`,bored:`You need a verb: 'was bored by'.`}},{id:`b1-tr-135`,type:`transform`,level:`B1`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Be quiet' is the opposite of 'make noise': Please be quiet in the library.`,original:`Please don't make so much noise.`,keyword:`QUIET`,template:`Please ___.`,solutions:[`be quiet`,`be quieter`,`be more quiet`],wrongReasons:{"be quietly":`After 'be' use the adjective 'quiet', not the adverb.`,quiet:`You need the verb 'be': 'be quiet'.`}}]};export{e as default};